  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Given that&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>}</mo></math></p><p>We need to find the number of symmetric matrices.</p><p>We know that a matrix is symmetric if&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#964;</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mtable columnalign=\"left\"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>13</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>21</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>22</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>23</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>31</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>32</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>33</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable></mfenced></math></p><p>Now&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>33</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;can have any digit from&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn></math>&nbsp;to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn></math>.</p><p>Number of ways this can be done is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></math></p><p>Now&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>21</mn></msub></math>. So number of ways this can be done is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>1</mn></math>.</p><p>Similarly&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>31</mn></msub></math>,&nbsp;So number of ways this can be done is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>1</mn></math>.</p><p>Also,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>23</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>32</mn></msub></math>. So number of ways this can be done is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>1</mn></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo></math>&nbsp;Total symmetric matrix<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></math></p><p>Hence this is the correct option.</p></blockquote>",
